:\nOceania:Australia-AU,Fiji-FJ\n\n\nRUBRIC:\nWhat is the name of the toy cowboy in Toy Story?Woody\nWhose nose grew longer every time he lied?Pinocchio\n\n\nSTUDENT:\n','manual',NULL,NULL,NULL),(14,'test','BRIEF:\n{\\rtf1\\ansi\\ansicpg1252\\cocoartf2822\n\\cocoatextscaling0\\cocoaplatform0{\\fonttbl\\f0\\fswiss\\fcharset0 Helvetica;}\n{\\colortbl;\\red255\\green255\\blue255;}\n{\\*\\expandedcolortbl;;}\n\\paperw11900\\paperh16840\\margl1440\\margr1440\\vieww11520\\viewh8400\\viewkind0\n\\pard\\tx566\\tx1133\\tx1700\\tx2267\\tx2834\\tx3401\\tx3968\\tx4535\\tx5102\\tx5669\\tx6236\\tx6803\\pardirnatural\\partightenfactor0\n\n\\f0\\fs24 \\cf0 \r\\\nRubric (100 marks)\r\\\nTechnical Accounting Understanding (30 marks)\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  26-30: Clear, accurate explanation of GRNI, accruals, balance sheet vs P&L treatment,\r\\\nand clearing process.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  20-25: Mostly correct with minor gaps.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  14-19: Basic understanding but lacks precision.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  0-13: Incorrect or confused.\r\\\nUnderstanding of GRNI Releases (20 marks)\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  17-20: Clearly explains releases, reasons for them, and distinguishes valid vs invalid\r\\\nuse.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  13-16: General understanding but limited depth.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  9-12: Superficial.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  0-8: Poor or incorrect.\r\\\nP&L Impact & Financial Insight (20 marks)\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  17-20: Strong understanding of how releases affect profit (cost reversal, timing, profit\r\\\ndistortion).\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  13-16: Directionally correct but not deep.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  9-12: Weak linkage.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  0-8: No clear understanding.\r\\\nAnalytical & Investigative Thinking (15 marks)\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  13-15: Logical, structured investigation (timing issues, missing invoices, over-accruals).\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  10-12: Some reasoning.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  6-9: Basic.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  0-5: Weak.\r\\\nCommercial Awareness (10 marks)\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  9-10: Connects GRNI to real risks (earnings manipulation, poor decisions, margin\r\\\ndistortion).\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  7-8: Some linkage.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  5-6: Limited.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  0-4: None.\r\\\nClarity & Communication (5 marks)\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  5: Clear, concise, structured.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  3-4: Mostly clear.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  1-2: Some confusion.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  0: Unclear.}\n\nRUBRIC:\n{\\rtf1\\ansi\\ansicpg1252\\cocoartf2822\n\\cocoatextscaling0\\cocoaplatform0{\\fonttbl\\f0\\fswiss\\fcharset0 Helvetica;}\n{\\colortbl;\\red255\\green255\\blue255;}\n{\\*\\expandedcolortbl;;}\n\\paperw11900\\paperh16840\\margl1440\\margr1440\\vieww11520\\viewh8400\\viewkind0\n\\pard\\tx566\\tx1133\\tx1700\\tx2267\\tx2834\\tx3401\\tx3968\\tx4535\\tx5102\\tx5669\\tx6236\\tx6803\\pardirnatural\\partightenfactor0\n\n\\f0\\fs24 \\cf0 \r\\\nRubric (100 marks)\r\\\nTechnical Accounting Understanding (30 marks)\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  26-30: Clear, accurate explanation of GRNI, accruals, balance sheet vs P&L treatment,\r\\\nand clearing process.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  20-25: Mostly correct with minor gaps.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  14-19: Basic understanding but lacks precision.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  0-13: Incorrect or confused.\r\\\nUnderstanding of GRNI Releases (20 marks)\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  17-20: Clearly explains releases, reasons for them, and distinguishes valid vs invalid\r\\\nuse.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  13-16: General understanding but limited depth.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  9-12: Superficial.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  0-8: Poor or incorrect.\r\\\nP&L Impact & Financial Insight (20 marks)\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  17-20: Strong understanding of how releases affect profit (cost reversal, timing, profit\r\\\ndistortion).\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  13-16: Directionally correct but not deep.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  9-12: Weak linkage.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  0-8: No clear understanding.\r\\\nAnalytical & Investigative Thinking (15 marks)\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  13-15: Logical, structured investigation (timing issues, missing invoices, over-accruals).\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  10-12: Some reasoning.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  6-9: Basic.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  0-5: Weak.\r\\\nCommercial Awareness (10 marks)\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  9-10: Connects GRNI to real risks (earnings manipulation, poor decisions, margin\r\\\ndistortion).\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  7-8: Some linkage.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  5-6: Limited.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  0-4: None.\r\\\nClarity & Communication (5 marks)\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  5: Clear, concise, structured.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  3-4: Mostly clear.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  1-2: Some confusion.\r\\\n\\uc0\\u9679  0: Unclear.}\n\nSTUDENT:\n','manual',NULL,NULL,NULL),(15,'test','BRIEF:\nQuestions\r\n“Can you explain what GRNI (Goods Received Not Invoiced) is?”\r\n“Why does GRNI exist in an FMCG business?”\r\n“What happens in the accounts when goods are received but the invoice hasn’t arrived yet?”\r\n“Where does GRNI sit on the balance sheet and how does it initially affect the P&L, if at all?”\r\n“What happens to GRNI when the supplier invoice is received?”\r\n“Under what circumstances would GRNI not clear properly?”\r\n“What is a GRNI release?”\r\n“Why might a company need to release GRNI balances?”\r\n“How does a GRNI release impact the P&L?”\r\n“Can you explain the difference between a justified release and an unjustified release?”\r\n“If a large GRNI balance is released at month-end, what could that indicate?”\r\n“How could this distort reported profit?”\r\n“What risks arise if GRNI releases are used aggressively?”\r\n“How might GRNI mismanagement affect decision-making in a commercial finance team?”\r\n“If you noticed an unusually high GRNI release this month, how would you investigate it?”\r\n“What controls would you put in place to prevent incorrect GRNI releases?”\r\n“Why is GRNI particularly important in FMCG businesses?”\r\n“How do high volumes and fast inventory turnover complicate GRNI management?”\r\n“Summarise how GRNI flows from goods receipt through to potential P&L impact.”\r\n“What would good practice look like for managing GRNI in a commercial finance function?”\r\n\r\n\n\nRUBRIC:\nQuestions\r\n“Can you explain what GRNI (Goods Received Not Invoiced) is?”\r\n“Why does GRNI exist in an FMCG business?”\r\n“What happens in the accounts when goods are received but the invoice hasn’t arrived yet?”\r\n“Where does GRNI sit on the balance sheet and how does it initially affect the P&L, if at all?”\r\n“What happens to GRNI when the supplier invoice is received?”\r\n“Under what circumstances would GRNI not clear properly?”\r\n“What is a GRNI release?”\r\n“Why might a company need to release GRNI balances?”\r\n“How does a GRNI release impact the P&L?”\r\n“Can you explain the difference between a justified release and an unjustified release?”\r\n“If a large GRNI balance is released at month-end, what could that indicate?”\r\n“How could this distort reported profit?”\r\n“What risks arise if GRNI releases are used aggressively?”\r\n“How might GRNI mismanagement affect decision-making in a commercial finance team?”\r\n“If you noticed an unusually high GRNI release this month, how would you investigate it?”\r\n“What controls would you put in place to prevent incorrect GRNI releases?”\r\n“Why is GRNI particularly important in FMCG businesses?”\r\n“How do high volumes and fast inventory turnover complicate GRNI management?”\r\n“Summarise how GRNI flows from goods receipt through to potential P&L impact.”\r\n“What would good practice look like for managing GRNI in a commercial finance function?”\r\n\r\n\n\nSTUDENT:\n','manual',NULL,NULL,NULL),(16,'test','BRIEF:\nQuestions\r\n“Can you explain what GRNI (Goods Received Not Invoiced) is?”\r\n“Why does GRNI exist in an FMCG business?”\r\n“What happens in the accounts when goods are received but the invoice hasn’t arrived yet?”\r\n“Where does GRNI sit on the balance sheet and how does it initially affect the P&L, if at all?”\r\n“What happens to GRNI when the supplier invoice is received?”\r\n“Under what circumstances would GRNI not clear properly?”\r\n