\n------------------------------------------------\nCRITERION 4: Strategic Vision and Cohesion\nWeight: 15%\n\n4 - Excellent\n    Understands that a strategy must be cohesive and scalable — not just technically\n    impressive in demos or niche applications. Can discuss what makes a vision\n    \"larger\" and commercially viable.\n\n3 - Proficient\n    Shows awareness that good demos or niche capabilities are not enough; some\n    understanding of the link between vision and commercial scale.\n\n2 - Developing\n    Recognises that vision matters but struggles to connect it to the lecture\'s\n    argument about commercial viability and strategic coherence.\n\n1 - Beginning\n    Little understanding of strategic vision beyond individual features or products.\n\n------------------------------------------------\nCRITERION 5: Critical Engagement with Source Material\nWeight: 10%\n\n4 - Excellent\n    Engages critically with the lecture content — e.g. acknowledges that the\n    speaker concedes competitors may have technical advantages, yet argues\n    strategic framing is what determines success. May raise counterpoints or\n    extend arguments beyond the transcript.\n\n3 - Proficient\n    Shows thoughtful engagement, not just summary. Some attempt to evaluate or\n    contextualise the ideas presented.\n\n2 - Developing\n    Largely descriptive. Restates lecture content without much analysis or\n    independent thought.\n\n1 - Beginning\n    Little to no evidence of critical engagement; response reads as a paraphrase\n    of the transcript.\n\n================================================\nSCORING SUMMARY\n\nCriterion                                  Weight   Score (1–4)   Weighted Score\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. Customer-First Strategy                  30%     ___           ___\n2. Application of Real-World Examples       25%     ___           ___\n3. Recognition of Technology-First Pitfall  20%     ___           ___\n4. Strategic Vision and Cohesion            15%     ___           ___\n5. Critical Engagement with Source Material 10%     ___           ___\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTOTAL                                      100%                   ___  / 4.0\n\nGrade conversion (if applicable):\n  3.5 – 4.0  →  A  (Excellent)\n  2.75 – 3.49 → B  (Proficient)\n  2.0 – 2.74 →  C  (Developing)\n  Below 2.0  →  D/F (Beginning)\n\n================================================\nASSESSOR NOTES\n--------------\n[Space for written feedback]\n\n\n\n\n================================================\n\n\nSTUDENT:\n','manual','','',13),(50,'Structural Features of Mitochondria','BRIEF:\nBIOSCI 301: Cell Biology and Molecular Mechanisms\nEssay Assignment Brief — Semester 1, 2026\n\nCourse: BIOSCI 301 — Cell Biology and Molecular Mechanisms\nAssessment Type: Written Essay\nWeighting: 15% of final grade\nWord Limit: 1,200–1,500 words (excluding references)\nDue Date: Friday 29 May 2026, 11:59pm\nSubmission: Via the course portal as a PDF\n\n---\n\nASSIGNMENT QUESTION\n\n\"The mitochondrion is often described as the powerhouse of the cell. Discuss the structural features of the mitochondrion and explain how these features support its role in ATP production through oxidative phosphorylation.\"\n\n---\n\nREQUIREMENTS\n\nYour essay must:\n- Describe the key structural features of the mitochondrion and link each feature explicitly to its functional role\n- Explain the electron transport chain (ETC), including the roles of NADH, FADH2, and molecular oxygen\n- Explain the chemiosmotic mechanism of ATP synthesis, including the role of the proton gradient and ATP synthase\n- Briefly compare oxidative phosphorylation with substrate-level phosphorylation in terms of ATP yield and mechanism\n- Be written in continuous prose (not dot points)\n- Include at least 4 references from peer-reviewed sources (lecture notes do not count)\n\n---\n\nMARKING\n\nThis essay will be marked against the provided rubric. Following submission, students will complete a short AI-driven oral assessment based on their essay. The oral assessment contributes a separate 20% weighting and is designed to verify understanding of the content in the submitted work.\n\n---\n\nACADEMIC INTEGRITY\n\nSubmitted work must be your own. Use of generative AI tools to write or substantially rewrite your essay is not permitted. You are expected to be able to discuss and defend the content of your submission in the oral assessment.\n\n\n\nRUBRIC:\nBIOSCI 301 — Essay Marking Rubric\nAssignment: Mitochondrial Structure and Oxidative Phosphorylation\n\nTotal marks: 100\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 1: Mitochondrial Structure and Function Link (25 marks)\n\nAssesses whether the student accurately describes structural features AND explicitly links each to function.\n\n25 (Excellent): Accurately describes outer membrane, inner membrane, cristae, matrix, and intermembrane space. Each feature is explicitly and correctly linked to its functional role (e.g. cristae → increased surface area for ETC; matrix → site of Krebs cycle and mtDNA; IMS → proton accumulation). Detail is precise and mechanistic.\n\n18–24 (Good): Most features described and linked to function. Minor gaps or imprecision in one or two areas. Overall understanding clear.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): Structures named but links to function are vague or incomplete. Some features omitted. Description is largely anatomical without mechanistic explanation.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Only superficial structural description. Little to no connection between structure and function.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 2: Electron Transport Chain (25 marks)\n\nAssesses accuracy and completeness of the ETC explanation.\n\n25 (Excellent): Correctly identifies Complexes I–IV and their roles. Accurately describes electron donors (NADH → Complex I; FADH2 → Complex II), electron carriers (CoQ, cytochrome c), and final electron acceptor (O2 → H2O at Complex IV). Correctly identifies which complexes pump protons. Explains that electron flow is coupled to proton translocation.\n\n18–24 (Good): ETC explained accurately with minor omissions (e.g. FADH2 entry point not specified, or CoQ not named). Core mechanism understood.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): General description of electrons moving along a chain and producing energy. Lacks specific detail on complexes, carriers, or proton pumping. O2 role may be vague.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Very limited understanding. May confuse ETC with other metabolic processes.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 3: Chemiosmotic Mechanism and ATP Synthesis (25 marks)\n\nAssesses understanding of the proton gradient and ATP synthase.\n\n25 (Excellent): Clearly explains the proton motive force (combining ΔpH and Δψ). Accurately describes proton flow through ATP synthase (F0F1 ATPase) and mechanical coupling to ATP synthesis. Correctly attributes the concept to Mitchell\'s chemiosmotic theory. May note that the proton gradient is generated by Complexes I, III, and IV.\n\n18–24 (Good): Proton gradient and ATP synthase explained correctly. Minor gaps (e.g. Δψ not distinguished from ΔpH, or Mitchell not named). Core mechanism sound.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): Understands that a proton gradient drives ATP synthesis but explanation is vague. ATP synthase may be mentioned without explanation of mechanism. Proton motive force not defined.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Some awareness that ATP is produced but mechanism largely absent or incorrect.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 4: Comparison with Substrate-Level Phosphorylation and ATP Yield (15 marks)\n\nAssesses ability to contextualise oxidative phosphorylation relative to other ATP-producing mechanisms.\n\n15 (Excellent): Accurately distinguishes oxidative phosphorylation (indirect, requires ETC and proton gradient, high yield) from substrate-level phosphorylation (direct phosphate transfer to ADP, no ETC required, low yield). Provides correct modern ATP yield estimates (~30–32 ATP per glucose). May note the updated yield vs the older 36–38 figure.\n\n10–14 (Good): Comparison is mostly accurate. May use older ATP yield figures (36–38) without acknowledgement. Distinction between mechanisms is clear.\n\n5–9 (Satisfactory): Comparison is present but superficial. May simply state that oxidative phosphorylation produces more ATP without explaining why.\n\n1–4 (Developing): Comparison attempted but largely inaccurate.\n\n0: No comparison made.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 5: Academic Writing and Referencing (10 marks)\n\n10: Well-structured, fluent prose. Correct and consistent use of scientific terminology. 4+ appropriate peer-reviewed references, correctly formatted.\n\n7–9: Generally clear writing with minor issues. Terminology mostly correct. References present, minor formatting issues.\n\n4–6: Some clarity issues. Terminology inconsistent. Fewer than 4 references or references not peer-reviewed.\n\n1–3: Poor structure and clarity. Significant terminology errors. Referencing largely absent or inappropriate.\n\n0: No references. Largely incoherent.\n\n---\n\nGRADE BOUNDARIES\n\n90–100: A+  80–89: A  70–79: A-  65–69: B+  60–64: B  50–59: C  40–49: D  0–39: F\n\n\n\nSTUDENT:\n','manual','','BIOSCI 301 — Weighted Areas of Focus & Global Questions\nInstructor Configuration for AI Oral Assessment\n\nLinked assignment: Mitochondrial Structure and Oxidative Phosphorylation Essay\n\n---\n\nWEIGHTED AREAS OF FOCUS\n\nThe AI oral assessor will allocate questioning time according to these weights, which reflect the instructor\'s priorities for the oral component.\n\nArea                                          | Weight | Notes\n----------------------------------------------|--------|----------------------------------------------\nETC mechanism (complexes, carriers, O2 role)  | 30%    | Most commonly misunderstood — probe deeply\nChemiosmotic theory & ATP synthase            | 30%    | Requires mechanistic reasoning beyond recall\nMitochondrial structure-function links        | 20%    | Check whether student understands WHY\nComparison with substrate-level phosph.       | 10%    | Check for surface-level vs genuine understanding\nReal-world / clinical application             | 10%    | Assess ability to apply concepts to new scenarios\n\n---\n\nGLOBAL QUESTIONS\n\nThese questions are asked regardless of essay content to assess integration and applied thinking.\n\nGQ1: \"Without looking at your essay — in your own words, what is the single most important thing the mitochondrion does and how does its structure make that possible?\"\n\nGQ2: \"If oxygen were suddenly unavailable to a cell, walk me through exactly what would happen to ATP production, step by step.\"\n\nGQ3: \"Your essay discusses the proton gradient. Can you explain what would happen if that gradient were artificially collapsed — say by a drug that made the inner membrane permeable to protons?\"\n\nGQ4: \"How would you explain the difference between the ETC and ATP synthase to someone who had never studied biology? What analogy might you use?\"\n\nGQ5: \"Is there anything in your essay that, having thought about it more, you think you could have explained more precisely or accurately?\"\n\n---\n\nINSTRUCTOR NOTES FOR AI ASSESSOR\n\n- This is an average cohort. Expect students to know the broad mechanism but struggle with specific details (e.g. which complexes pump protons, the distinction between ΔpH and Δψ).\n- Do not penalise use of the older 36–38 ATP figure if the student demonstrates understanding of why the estimate exists.\n- If a student cannot answer a question, offer one prompt/hint before moving on. Note whether the hint was needed.\n- Avoid leading questions. If a student\'s answer is partially correct, probe the gap rather than confirming the correct part.\n- The oral is not a re-examination of the essay — it is a verification of understanding. Focus on whether the student can reason, not just recall.\n\n',13),(51,'Structural Features of Mitochondria','BRIEF:\nBIOSCI 301: Cell Biology and Molecular Mechanisms\nEssay Assignment Brief — Semester 1, 2026\n\nCourse: BIOSCI 301 — Cell Biology and Molecular Mechanisms\nAssessment Type: Written Essay\nWeighting: 15% of final grade\nWord Limit: 1,200–1,500 words (excluding references)\nDue Date: Friday 29 May 2026, 11:59pm\nSubmission: Via the course portal as a PDF\n\n---\n\nASSIGNMENT QUESTION\n\n\"The mitochondrion is often described as the powerhouse of the cell. Discuss the structural features of the mitochondrion and explain how these features support its role in ATP production through oxidative phosphorylation.\"\n\n---\n\nREQUIREMENTS\n\nYour essay must:\n- Describe the key structural features of the mitochondrion and link each feature explicitly to its functional role\n- Explain the electron transport chain (ETC), including the roles of NADH, FADH2, and molecular oxygen\n- Explain the chemiosmotic mechanism of ATP synthesis, including the role of the proton gradient and ATP synthase\n- Briefly compare oxidative phosphorylation with substrate-level phosphorylation in terms of ATP yield and mechanism\n- Be written in continuous prose (not dot points)\n- Include at least 4 references from peer-reviewed sources (lecture notes do not count)\n\n---\n\nMARKING\n\nThis essay will be marked against the provided rubric. Following submission, students will complete a short AI-driven oral assessment based on their essay. The oral assessment contributes a separate 20% weighting and is designed to verify understanding of the content in the submitted work.\n\n---\n\nACADEMIC INTEGRITY\n\nSubmitted work must be your own. Use of generative AI tools to write or substantially rewrite your essay is not permitted. You are expected to be able to discuss and defend the content of your submission in the oral assessment.\n\n\n\nRUBRIC:\nBIOSCI 301 — Essay Marking Rubric\nAssignment: Mitochondrial Structure and Oxidative Phosphorylation\n\nTotal marks: 100\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 1: Mitochondrial Structure and Function Link (25 marks)\n\nAssesses whether the student accurately describes structural features AND explicitly links each to function.\n\n25 (Excellent): Accurately describes outer membrane, inner membrane, cristae, matrix, and intermembrane space. Each feature is explicitly and correctly linked to its functional role (e.g. cristae → increased surface area for ETC; matrix → site of Krebs cycle and mtDNA; IMS → proton accumulation). Detail is precise and mechanistic.\n\n18–24 (Good): Most features described and linked to function. Minor gaps or imprecision in one or two areas. Overall understanding clear.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): Structures named but links to function are vague or incomplete. Some features omitted. Description is largely anatomical without mechanistic explanation.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Only superficial structural description. Little to no connection between structure and function.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 2: Electron Transport Chain (25 marks)\n\nAssesses accuracy and completeness of the ETC explanation.\n\n25 (Excellent): Correctly identifies Complexes I–IV and their roles. Accurately describes electron donors (NADH → Complex I; FADH2 → Complex II), electron carriers (CoQ, cytochrome c), and final electron acceptor (O2 → H2O at Complex IV). Correctly identifies which complexes pump protons. Explains that electron flow is coupled to proton translocation.\n\n18–24 (Good): ETC explained accurately with minor omissions (e.g. FADH2 entry point not specified, or CoQ not named). Core mechanism understood.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): General description of electrons moving along a chain and producing energy. Lacks specific detail on complexes, carriers, or proton pumping. O2 role may be vague.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Very limited understanding. May confuse ETC with other metabolic processes.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 3: Chemiosmotic Mechanism and ATP Synthesis (25 marks)\n\nAssesses understanding of the proton gradient and ATP synthase.\n\n25 (Excellent): Clearly explains the proton motive force (combining ΔpH and Δψ). Accurately describes proton flow through ATP synthase (F0F1 ATPase) and mechanical coupling to ATP synthesis. Correctly attributes the concept to Mitchell\'s chemiosmotic theory. May note that the proton gradient is generated by Complexes I, III, and IV.\n\n18–24 (Good): Proton gradient and ATP synthase explained correctly. Minor gaps (e.g. Δψ not distinguished from ΔpH, or Mitchell not named). Core mechanism sound.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): Understands that a proton gradient drives ATP synthesis but explanation is vague. ATP synthase may be mentioned without explanation of mechanism. Proton motive force not defined.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Some awareness that ATP is produced but mechanism largely absent or incorrect.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 4: Comparison with Substrate-Level Phosphorylation and ATP Yield (15 marks)\n\nAssesses ability to contextualise oxidative phosphorylation relative to other ATP-producing mechanisms.\n\n15 (Excellent): Accurately distinguishes oxidative phosphorylation (indirect, requires ETC and proton gradient, high yield) from substrate-level phosphorylation (direct phosphate transfer to ADP, no ETC required, low yield). Provides correct modern ATP yield estimates (~30–32 ATP per glucose). May note the updated yield vs the older 36–38 figure.\n\n10–14 (Good): Comparison is mostly accurate. May use older ATP yield figures (36–38) without acknowledgement. Distinction between mechanisms is clear.\n\n5–9 (Satisfactory): Comparison is present but superficial. May simply state that oxidative phosphorylation produces more ATP without explaining why.\n\n1–4 (Developing): Comparison attempted but largely inaccurate.\n\n0: No comparison made.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 5: Academic Writing and Referencing (10 marks)\n\n10: Well-structured, fluent prose. Correct and consistent use of scientific terminology. 4+ appropriate peer-reviewed references, correctly formatted.\n\n7–9: Generally clear writing with minor issues. Terminology mostly correct. References present, minor formatting issues.\n\n4–6: Some clarity issues. Terminology inconsistent. Fewer than 4 references or references not peer-reviewed.\n\n1–3: Poor structure and clarity. Significant terminology errors. Referencing largely absent or inappropriate.\n\n0: No references. Largely incoherent.\n\n---\n\nGRADE BOUNDARIES\n\n90–100: A+  80–89: A  70–79: A-  65–69: B+  60–64: B  50–59: C  40–49: D  0–39: F\n\n\n\nSTUDENT:\n','manual','','BIOSCI 301 — Weighted Areas of Focus & Global Questions\nInstructor Configuration for AI Oral Assessment\n\nLinked assignment: Mitochondrial Structure and Oxidative Phosphorylation Essay\n\n---\n\nWEIGHTED AREAS OF FOCUS\n\nThe AI oral assessor will allocate questioning time according to these weights, which reflect the instructor\'s priorities for the oral component.\n\nArea                                          | Weight | Notes\n----------------------------------------------|--------|----------------------------------------------\nETC mechanism (complexes, carriers, O2 role)  | 30%    | Most commonly misunderstood — probe deeply\nChemiosmotic theory & ATP synthase            | 30%    | Requires mechanistic reasoning beyond recall\nMitochondrial structure-function links        | 20%    | Check whether student understands WHY\nComparison with substrate-level phosph.       | 10%    | Check for surface-level vs genuine understanding\nReal-world / clinical application             | 10%    | Assess ability to apply concepts to new scenarios\n\n---\n\nGLOBAL QUESTIONS\n\nThese questions are asked regardless of essay content to assess integration and applied thinking.\n\nGQ1: \"Without looking at your essay — in your own words, what is the single most important thing the mitochondrion does and how does its structure make that possible?\"\n\nGQ2: \"If oxygen were suddenly unavailable to a cell, walk me through exactly what would happen to ATP production, step by step.\"\n\nGQ3: \"Your essay discusses the proton gradient. Can you explain what would happen if that gradient were artificially collapsed — say by a drug that made the inner membrane permeable to protons?\"\n\nGQ4: \"How would you explain the difference between the ETC and ATP synthase to someone who had never studied biology? What analogy might you use?\"\n\nGQ5: \"Is there anything in your essay that, having thought about it more, you think you could have explained more precisely or accurately?\"\n\n---\n\nINSTRUCTOR NOTES FOR AI ASSESSOR\n\n- This is an average cohort. Expect students to know the broad mechanism but struggle with specific details (e.g. which complexes pump protons, the distinction between ΔpH and Δψ).\n- Do not penalise use of the older 36–38 ATP figure if the student demonstrates understanding of why the estimate exists.\n- If a student cannot answer a question, offer one prompt/hint before moving on. Note whether the hint was needed.\n- Avoid leading questions. If a student\'s answer is partially correct, probe the gap rather than confirming the correct part.\n- The oral is not a re-examination of the essay — it is a verification of understanding. Focus on whether the student can reason, not just recall.\n\n',14),(52,'BIOSCI 301 Assignment #1','BRIEF:\nBIOSCI 301: Cell Biology and Molecular Mechanisms\nEssay Assignment Brief — Semester 1, 2026\n\nCourse: BIOSCI 301 — Cell Biology and Molecular Mechanisms\nAssessment Type: Written Essay\nWeighting: 15% of final grade\nWord Limit: 1,200–1,500 words (excluding references)\nDue Date: Friday 29 May 2026, 11:59pm\nSubmission: Via the course portal as a PDF\n\n---\n\nASSIGNMENT QUESTION\n\n\"The mitochondrion is often described as the powerhouse of the cell. Discuss the structural features of the mitochondrion and explain how these features support its role in ATP production through oxidative phosphorylation.\"\n\n---\n\nREQUIREMENTS\n\nYour essay must:\n- Describe the key structural features of the mitochondrion and link each feature explicitly to its functional role\n- Explain the electron transport chain (ETC), including the roles of NADH, FADH2, and molecular oxygen\n- Explain the chemiosmotic mechanism of ATP synthesis, including the role of the proton gradient and ATP synthase\n- Briefly compare oxidative phosphorylation with substrate-level phosphorylation in terms of ATP yield and mechanism\n- Be written in continuous prose (not dot points)\n- Include at least 4 references from peer-reviewed sources (lecture notes do not count)\n\n---\n\nMARKING\n\nThis essay will be marked against the provided rubric. Following submission, students will complete a short AI-driven oral assessment based on their essay. The oral assessment contributes a separate 20% weighting and is designed to verify understanding of the content in the submitted work.\n\n---\n\nACADEMIC INTEGRITY\n\nSubmitted work must be your own. Use of generative AI tools to write or substantially rewrite your essay is not permitted. You are expected to be able to discuss and defend the content of your submission in the oral assessment.\n\n\n\nRUBRIC:\nBIOSCI 301 — Essay Marking Rubric\nAssignment: Mitochondrial Structure and Oxidative Phosphorylation\n\nTotal marks: 100\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 1: Mitochondrial Structure and Function Link (25 marks)\n\nAssesses whether the student accurately describes structural features AND explicitly links each to function.\n\n25 (Excellent): Accurately describes outer membrane, inner membrane, cristae, matrix, and intermembrane space. Each feature is explicitly and correctly linked to its functional role (e.g. cristae → increased surface area for ETC; matrix → site of Krebs cycle and mtDNA; IMS → proton accumulation). Detail is precise and mechanistic.\n\n18–24 (Good): Most features described and linked to function. Minor gaps or imprecision in one or two areas. Overall understanding clear.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): Structures named but links to function are vague or incomplete. Some features omitted. Description is largely anatomical without mechanistic explanation.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Only superficial structural description. Little to no connection between structure and function.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 2: Electron Transport Chain (25 marks)\n\nAssesses accuracy and completeness of the ETC explanation.\n\n25 (Excellent): Correctly identifies Complexes I–IV and their roles. Accurately describes electron donors (NADH → Complex I; FADH2 → Complex II), electron carriers (CoQ, cytochrome c), and final electron acceptor (O2 → H2O at Complex IV). Correctly identifies which complexes pump protons. Explains that electron flow is coupled to proton translocation.\n\n18–24 (Good): ETC explained accurately with minor omissions (e.g. FADH2 entry point not specified, or CoQ not named). Core mechanism understood.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): General description of electrons moving along a chain and producing energy. Lacks specific detail on complexes, carriers, or proton pumping. O2 role may be vague.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Very limited understanding. May confuse ETC with other metabolic processes.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 3: Chemiosmotic Mechanism and ATP Synthesis (25 marks)\n\nAssesses understanding of the proton gradient and ATP synthase.\n\n25 (Excellent): Clearly explains the proton motive force (combining ΔpH and Δψ). Accurately describes proton flow through ATP synthase (F0F1 ATPase) and mechanical coupling to ATP synthesis. Correctly attributes the concept to Mitchell\'s chemiosmotic theory. May note that the proton gradient is generated by Complexes I, III, and IV.\n\n18–24 (Good): Proton gradient and ATP synthase explained correctly. Minor gaps (e.g. Δψ not distinguished from ΔpH, or Mitchell not named). Core mechanism sound.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): Understands that a proton gradient drives ATP synthesis but explanation is vague. ATP synthase may be mentioned without explanation of mechanism. Proton motive force not defined.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Some awareness that ATP is produced but mechanism largely absent or incorrect.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 4: Comparison with Substrate-Level Phosphorylation and ATP Yield (15 marks)\n\nAssesses ability to contextualise oxidative phosphorylation relative to other ATP-producing mechanisms.\n\n15 (Excellent): Accurately distinguishes oxidative phosphorylation (indirect, requires ETC and proton gradient, high yield) from substrate-level phosphorylation (direct phosphate transfer to ADP, no ETC required, low yield). Provides correct modern ATP yield estimates (~30–32 ATP per glucose). May note the updated yield vs the older 36–38 figure.\n\n10–14 (Good): Comparison is mostly accurate. May use older ATP yield figures (36–38) without acknowledgement. Distinction between mechanisms is clear.\n\n5–9 (Satisfactory): Comparison is present but superficial. May simply state that oxidative phosphorylation produces more ATP without explaining why.\n\n1–4 (Developing): Comparison attempted but largely inaccurate.\n\n0: No comparison made.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 5: Academic Writing and Referencing (10 marks)\n\n10: Well-structured, fluent prose. Correct and consistent use of scientific terminology. 4+ appropriate peer-reviewed references, correctly formatted.\n\n7–9: Generally clear writing with minor issues. Terminology mostly correct. References present, minor formatting issues.\n\n4–6: Some clarity issues. Terminology inconsistent. Fewer than 4 references or references not peer-reviewed.\n\n1–3: Poor structure and clarity. Significant terminology errors. Referencing largely absent or inappropriate.\n\n0: No references. Largely incoherent.\n\n---\n\nGRADE BOUNDARIES\n\n90–100: A+  80–89: A  70–79: A-  65–69: B+  60–64: B  50–59: C  40–49: D  0–39: F\n\n\n\nSTUDENT:\n','manual','','',15),(53,'BIOSCI Assignment #2','BRIEF:\nBIOSCI 301: Cell Biology and Molecular Mechanisms\nEssay Assignment Brief — Semester 1, 2026\n\nCourse: BIOSCI 301 — Cell Biology and Molecular Mechanisms\nAssessment Type: Written Essay\nWeighting: 15% of final grade\nWord Limit: 1,200–1,500 words (excluding references)\nDue Date: Friday 29 May 2026, 11:59pm\nSubmission: Via the course portal as a PDF\n\n---\n\nASSIGNMENT QUESTION\n\n\"The mitochondrion is often described as the powerhouse of the cell. Discuss the structural features of the mitochondrion and explain how these features support its role in ATP production through oxidative phosphorylation.\"\n\n---\n\nREQUIREMENTS\n\nYour essay must:\n- Describe the key structural features of the mitochondrion and link each feature explicitly to its functional role\n- Explain the electron transport chain (ETC), including the roles of NADH, FADH2, and molecular oxygen\n- Explain the chemiosmotic mechanism of ATP synthesis, including the role of the proton gradient and ATP synthase\n- Briefly compare oxidative phosphorylation with substrate-level phosphorylation in terms of ATP yield and mechanism\n- Be written in continuous prose (not dot points)\n- Include at least 4 references from peer-reviewed sources (lecture notes do not count)\n\n---\n\nMARKING\n\nThis essay will be marked against the provided rubric. Following submission, students will complete a short AI-driven oral assessment based on their essay. The oral assessment contributes a separate 20% weighting and is designed to verify understanding of the content in the submitted work.\n\n---\n\nACADEMIC INTEGRITY\n\nSubmitted work must be your own. Use of generative AI tools to write or substantially rewrite your essay is not permitted. You are expected to be able to discuss and defend the content of your submission in the oral assessment.\n\n\n\nRUBRIC:\nBIOSCI 301 — Essay Marking Rubric\nAssignment: Mitochondrial Structure and Oxidative Phosphorylation\n\nTotal m