 (25 marks)\n\nAssesses understanding of the proton gradient and ATP synthase.\n\n25 (Excellent): Clearly explains the proton motive force (combining ΔpH and Δψ). Accurately describes proton flow through ATP synthase (F0F1 ATPase) and mechanical coupling to ATP synthesis. Correctly attributes the concept to Mitchell\'s chemiosmotic theory. May note that the proton gradient is generated by Complexes I, III, and IV.\n\n18–24 (Good): Proton gradient and ATP synthase explained correctly. Minor gaps (e.g. Δψ not distinguished from ΔpH, or Mitchell not named). Core mechanism sound.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): Understands that a proton gradient drives ATP synthesis but explanation is vague. ATP synthase may be mentioned without explanation of mechanism. Proton motive force not defined.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Some awareness that ATP is produced but mechanism largely absent or incorrect.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 4: Comparison with Substrate-Level Phosphorylation and ATP Yield (15 marks)\n\nAssesses ability to contextualise oxidative phosphorylation relative to other ATP-producing mechanisms.\n\n15 (Excellent): Accurately distinguishes oxidative phosphorylation (indirect, requires ETC and proton gradient, high yield) from substrate-level phosphorylation (direct phosphate transfer to ADP, no ETC required, low yield). Provides correct modern ATP yield estimates (~30–32 ATP per glucose). May note the updated yield vs the older 36–38 figure.\n\n10–14 (Good): Comparison is mostly accurate. May use older ATP yield figures (36–38) without acknowledgement. Distinction between mechanisms is clear.\n\n5–9 (Satisfactory): Comparison is present but superficial. May simply state that oxidative phosphorylation produces more ATP without explaining why.\n\n1–4 (Developing): Comparison attempted but largely inaccurate.\n\n0: No comparison made.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 5: Academic Writing and Referencing (10 marks)\n\n10: Well-structured, fluent prose. Correct and consistent use of scientific terminology. 4+ appropriate peer-reviewed references, correctly formatted.\n\n7–9: Generally clear writing with minor issues. Terminology mostly correct. References present, minor formatting issues.\n\n4–6: Some clarity issues. Terminology inconsistent. Fewer than 4 references or references not peer-reviewed.\n\n1–3: Poor structure and clarity. Significant terminology errors. Referencing largely absent or inappropriate.\n\n0: No references. Largely incoherent.\n\n---\n\nGRADE BOUNDARIES\n\n90–100: A+  80–89: A  70–79: A-  65–69: B+  60–64: B  50–59: C  40–49: D  0–39: F\n\n\n\nSTUDENT:\n','manual','','',15),(54,'BUS 251 Essay Review','BRIEF:\nBUS 251: Principles of Economics\nEssay Assignment Brief — Semester 1, 2026\n\nCourse: BUS 251 — Principles of Economics\nAssessment Type: Written Essay\nWeighting: 15% of final grade\nWord Limit: 1,200–1,500 words (excluding references)\nDue Date: Friday 29 May 2026, 11:59pm\nSubmission: Via the course portal as a PDF\n\n---\n\nASSIGNMENT QUESTION\n\n\"Using supply and demand analysis, explain how a significant external shock — such as a sudden increase in input costs or a major shift in consumer preferences — affects market equilibrium. In your answer, discuss the roles of the price mechanism, elasticity, and government intervention in restoring or disrupting equilibrium.\"\n\n---\n\nREQUIREMENTS\n\nYour essay must:\n- Define and explain market equilibrium and how it is determined by supply and demand\n- Analyse how an external shock shifts either the supply or demand curve, with reference to a real-world example\n- Explain the role of the price mechanism in moving the market toward a new equilibrium\n- Discuss how price elasticity of supply and/or demand affects the magnitude of price and quantity changes following a shock\n- Evaluate at least one form of government intervention (e.g. price ceiling, price floor, subsidy, or tax) and its effect on market outcomes\n- Be written in continuous prose (not dot points or bullet lists)\n- Include at least 4 references from peer-reviewed sources or reputable economic publications (lecture notes do not count)\n\n---\n\nMARKING\n\nThis essay will be marked against the provided rubric. Following submission, students will complete a short AI-driven oral assessment based on their submitted essay. The oral assessment contributes a separate 20% weighting and is designed to verify that students genuinely understand the content they have submitted.\n\n---\n\nACADEMIC INTEGRITY\n\nSubmitted work must be your own. Use of generative AI tools to write or substantially rewrite your essay is not permitted. You are expected to be able to discuss and defend the content of your submission in the oral assessment.\n\n\n\nRUBRIC:\nBUS 251 — Essay Marking Rubric\nAssignment: Supply and Demand Analysis — External Shocks and Market Equilibrium\n\nTotal marks: 100\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 1: Understanding of Market Equilibrium and the Price Mechanism (25 marks)\n\nAssesses whether the student accurately defines equilibrium and explains how the price mechanism operates following a shock.\n\n25 (Excellent): Precisely defines equilibrium as the price at which quantity supplied equals quantity demanded. Clearly explains how a shock creates a surplus or shortage, and how the price mechanism — through price signals and incentives — moves the market toward a new equilibrium. Correctly identifies the direction of curve shifts and resulting changes in equilibrium price and quantity.\n\n18–24 (Good): Equilibrium and price mechanism explained accurately with minor imprecision. Curve shifts correctly identified. Core mechanism understood.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): General understanding of equilibrium present but explanation of the price mechanism is vague. Curve shifts may be described without explaining the adjustment process. Real-world example present but loosely connected.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Equilibrium defined superficially or incorrectly. Limited understanding of how prices signal and coordinate market activity.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 2: Analysis of an External Shock (25 marks)\n\nAssesses the quality of the student\'s analysis of a specific real-world supply or demand shock.\n\n25 (Excellent): Selects a specific, relevant real-world example. Clearly identifies whether the shock affects supply or demand (or both) and explains the underlying mechanism (e.g. why rising energy costs shift the supply curve left). Correctly predicts the direction of change in equilibrium price and quantity. Discusses any secondary effects or feedback mechanisms.\n\n18–24 (Good): Example is relevant and analysis is mostly accurate. Shock correctly identified as supply- or demand-side. Minor gaps in explaining the mechanism.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): Example is present but analysis is superficial. Curve shift identified but mechanism not well explained. Predicted outcomes may be partially correct.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Example vague or inappropriate. Analysis largely absent or incorrect.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 3: Application of Elasticity (25 marks)\n\nAssesses the student\'s ability to apply price elasticity concepts to modify their analysis.\n\n25 (Excellent): Correctly defines price elasticity of demand and/or supply. Accurately explains how elasticity affects the relative magnitude of price vs quantity changes following a shock (e.g. inelastic demand → larger price change, smaller quantity change). Applies elasticity correctly to their chosen example. May distinguish between short-run and long-run elasticity.\n\n18–24 (Good): Elasticity defined and applied correctly with minor gaps. Impact on price and quantity changes understood. Short-run vs long-run distinction may be absent.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): Elasticity mentioned and broadly defined but application to the example is vague or incomplete. May correctly state that inelastic demand leads to larger price changes without explaining why.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Elasticity mentioned but defined incorrectly or not applied to the analysis.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 4: Evaluation of Government Intervention (15 marks)\n\nAssesses the student\'s ability to evaluate a policy response and its market consequences.\n\n15 (Excellent): Selects a specific intervention (price ceiling, floor, subsidy, or tax) and accurately explains its intended purpose and effect on market outcomes. Correctly identifies deadweight loss, shortages, or surpluses created by the intervention. Evaluates trade-offs — acknowledges both intended benefits and unintended consequences. May reference a real policy example.\n\n10–14 (Good): Intervention explained correctly with mostly accurate effects. Trade-offs acknowledged. Minor gaps in analysis of unintended consequences.\n\n5–9 (Satisfactory): Intervention identified and described but analysis of effects is superficial. Deadweight loss or market distortion may not be discussed.\n\n1–4 (Developing): Intervention mentioned but largely misunderstood or incorrectly analysed.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 5: Academic Writing and Referencing (10 marks)\n\n10: Well-structured, fluent prose with a clear argument. Correct and consistent use of economic terminology. 4+ appropriate references, correctly formatted.\n\n7–9: Generally clear writing with minor issues. Terminology mostly correct. References present with minor formatting issues.\n\n4–6: Some clarity issues. Terminology inconsistent. Fewer than 4 references or sources of questionable quality.\n\n1–3: Poor structure and clarity. Significant terminology errors. Referencing largely absent.\n\n0: No references. Largely incoherent.\n\n---\n\nGRADE BOUNDARIES\n\n90–100: A+  80–89: A  70–79: A-  65–69: B+  60–64: B  50–59: C  40–49: D  0–39: F\n\n\n\nSTUDENT:\n','manual','','BUS 251 — Weighted Areas of Focus & Global Questions\nInstructor Configuration for AI Oral Assessment\n\nLinked assignment: Supply and Demand Analysis — External Shocks and Market Equilibrium\n\n---\n\nWEIGHTED AREAS OF FOCUS\n\nArea                                          | Weight | Notes\n----------------------------------------------|--------|----------------------------------------------\nPrice mechanism & equilibrium adjustment      | 30%    | Core concept — probe whether student understands adjustment process, not just the definition\nElasticity and its effect on outcomes         | 30%    | Commonly misapplied — check student can reason through price vs quantity tradeoffs\nExternal shock analysis                       | 20%    | Check whether student can apply framework to a novel example, not just their chosen one\nGovernment intervention & market distortions  | 20%    | Probe unintended consequences and deadweight loss specifically\n\n---\n\nGLOBAL QUESTIONS\n\nGQ1: \"Without referencing your essay — if the price of coffee beans doubled overnight, walk me through what would happen in the retail coffee market step by step.\"\n\nGQ2: \"You discuss equilibrium in your essay. Can you explain what it means for a market to be \'out of equilibrium\' and what forces bring it back?\"\n\nGQ3: \"If the government introduced a price ceiling on rental housing below the current market price, what would happen to the quantity of housing supplied and demanded? Who benefits and who loses?\"\n\nGQ4: \"How would your analysis change if the good you discussed had perfectly inelastic demand? What about perfectly elastic demand?\"\n\nGQ5: \"Is there anything in your essay where, thinking about it now, you feel your analysis could have been more precise or considered a factor you left out?\"\n\n---\n\nINSTRUCTOR NOTES FOR AI ASSESSOR\n\n- This is a first/second year economics course. Students are expected to understand core supply and demand concepts but are not expected to use mathematical models or formal derivations.\n- Diagrams cannot be assessed in the oral format — ask students to describe what a diagram would look like rather than draw one.\n- Common weaknesses in this cohort: conflating a movement along a curve with a shift of the curve; incorrectly applying elasticity (e.g. saying elastic demand means bigger price changes); and describing government intervention without analysing unintended consequences.\n- If a student cannot answer a question, offer one prompt before moving on. Note whether the hint was needed.\n- The oral is a verification of understanding, not a repeat of the essay. Prioritise questions that probe reasoning, not recall.\n\n',16),(55,'BIOSCI 301 Grade Confirmation','BRIEF:\nBIOSCI 301: Cell Biology and Molecular Mechanisms\nEssay Assignment Brief — Semester 1, 2026\n\nCourse: BIOSCI 301 — Cell Biology and Molecular Mechanisms\nAssessment Type: Written Essay\nWeighting: 15% of final grade\nWord Limit: 1,200–1,500 words (excluding references)\nDue Date: Friday 29 May 2026, 11:59pm\nSubmission: Via the course portal as a PDF\n\n---\n\nASSIGNMENT QUESTION\n\n\"The mitochondrion is often described as the powerhouse of the cell. Discuss the structural features of the mitochondrion and explain how these features support its role in ATP production through oxidative phosphorylation.\"\n\n---\n\nREQUIREMENTS\n\nYour essay must:\n- Describe the key structural features of the mitochondrion and link each feature explicitly to its functional role\n- Explain the electron transport chain (ETC), including the roles of NADH, FADH2, and molecular oxygen\n- Explain the chemiosmotic mechanism of ATP synthesis, including the role of the proton gradient and ATP synthase\n- Briefly compare oxidative phosphorylation with substrate-level phosphorylation in terms of ATP yield and mechanism\n- Be written in continuous prose (not dot points)\n- Include at least 4 references from peer-reviewed sources (lecture notes do not count)\n\n---\n\nMARKING\n\nThis essay will be marked against the provided rubric. Following submission, students will complete a short AI-driven oral assessment based on their essay. The oral assessment contributes a separate 20% weighting and is designed to verify understanding of the content in the submitted work.\n\n---\n\nACADEMIC INTEGRITY\n\nSubmitted work must be your own. Use of generative AI tools to write or substantially rewrite your essay is not permitted. You are expected to be able to discuss and defend the content of your submission in the oral assessment.\n\n\n\nRUBRIC:\nBIOSCI 301 — Essay Marking Rubric\nAssignment: Mitochondrial Structure and Oxidative Phosphorylation\n\nTotal marks: 100\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 1: Mitochondrial Structure and Function Link (25 marks)\n\nAssesses whether the student accurately describes structural features AND explicitly links each to function.\n\n25 (Excellent): Accurately describes outer membrane, inner membrane, cristae, matrix, and intermembrane space. Each feature is explicitly and correctly linked to its functional role (e.g. cristae → increased surface area for ETC; matrix → site of Krebs cycle and mtDNA; IMS → proton accumulation). Detail is precise and mechanistic.\n\n18–24 (Good): Most features described and linked to function. Minor gaps or imprecision in one or two areas. Overall understanding clear.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): Structures named but links to function are vague or incomplete. Some features omitted. Description is largely anatomical without mechanistic explanation.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Only superficial structural description. Little to no connection between structure and function.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 2: Electron Transport Chain (25 marks)\n\nAssesses accuracy and completeness of the ETC explanation.\n\n25 (Excellent): Correctly identifies Complexes I–IV and their roles. Accurately describes electron donors (NADH → Complex I; FADH2 → Complex II), electron carriers (CoQ, cytochrome c), and final electron acceptor (O2 → H2O at Complex IV). Correctly identifies which complexes pump protons. Explains that electron flow is coupled to proton translocation.\n\n18–24 (Good): ETC explained accurately with minor omissions (e.g. FADH2 entry point not specified, or CoQ not named). Core mechanism understood.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): General description of electrons moving along a chain and producing energy. Lacks specific detail on complexes, carriers, or proton pumping. O2 role may be vague.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Very limited understanding. May confuse ETC with other metabolic processes.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 3: Chemiosmotic Mechanism and ATP Synthesis (25 marks)\n\nAssesses understanding of the proton gradient and ATP synthase.\n\n25 (Excellent): Clearly explains the proton motive force (combining ΔpH and Δψ). Accurately describes proton flow through ATP synthase (F0F1 ATPase) and mechanical coupling to ATP synthesis. Correctly attributes the concept to Mitchell\'s chemiosmotic theory. May note that the proton gradient is generated by Complexes I, III, and IV.\n\n18–24 (Good): Proton gradient and ATP synthase explained correctly. Minor gaps (e.g. Δψ not distinguished from ΔpH, or Mitchell not named). Core mechanism sound.\n\n10–17 (Satisfactory): Understands that a proton gradient drives ATP synthesis but explanation is vague. ATP synthase may be mentioned without explanation of mechanism. Proton motive force not defined.\n\n1–9 (Developing): Some awareness that ATP is produced but mechanism largely absent or incorrect.\n\n0: No relevant content.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 4: Comparison with Substrate-Level Phosphorylation and ATP Yield (15 marks)\n\nAssesses ability to contextualise oxidative phosphorylation relative to other ATP-producing mechanisms.\n\n15 (Excellent): Accurately distinguishes oxidative phosphorylation (indirect, requires ETC and proton gradient, high yield) from substrate-level phosphorylation (direct phosphate transfer to ADP, no ETC required, low yield). Provides correct modern ATP yield estimates (~30–32 ATP per glucose). May note the updated yield vs the older 36–38 figure.\n\n10–14 (Good): Comparison is mostly accurate. May use older ATP yield figures (36–38) without acknowledgement. Distinction between mechanisms is clear.\n\n5–9 (Satisfactory): Comparison is present but superficial. May simply state that oxidative phosphorylation produces more ATP without explaining why.\n\n1–4 (Developing): Comparison attempted but largely inaccurate.\n\n0: No comparison made.\n\n---\n\nCRITERION 5: Academic Writing and Referencing (10 marks)\n\n10: Well-structured, fluent prose. Correct and consistent use of scientific terminology. 4+ appropriate peer-reviewed references, correctly formatted.\n\n7–9: Generally clear writing with minor issues. Terminology mostly correct. References present, minor formatting issues.\n\n4–6: Some clarity issues. Terminology inconsistent. Fewer than 4 references or references not peer-reviewed.\n\n1–3: Poor structure and clarity. Significant terminology errors. Referencing largely absent or inappropriate.\n\n0: No references. Largely incoherent.\n\n---\n\nGRADE BOUNDARIES\n\n90–100: A+  80–89: A  70–79: A-  65–69: B+  60–64: B  50–59: C  40–49: D  0–39: F\n\n\n\nSTUDENT:\n','manual','','',15),(56,'Photosynthesis Fundamentals (Known-Good Test)','Photosynthesis','manual','','[{\"text\": \"Ask the student to explain the overall purpose of photosynthesis, including the main inputs and outputs and, if they can, the overall balanced equation.\", \"weight\": 30, \"_comment\": \"Core process\"}, {\"text\": \"Prompt the student to describe the two main stages of photosynthesis - the light-dependent reactions and the Calvin cycle - including where in the chloroplast each takes place and how they are connected.\", \"weight\": 25, \"_comment\": \"Two stages\"}, {\"text\": \"Inquire about the role of chlorophyll and other pigments in capturing light energy, why plants appear green, and what happens to the absorbed energy.\", \"weight\": 20, \"_comment\": \"Pigments and light\"}, {\"text\": \"Ask the student to discuss the factors that affect the rate of photosynthesis - such as light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration, and temperature - and what is meant by a limiting factor.\", \"weight\": 15, \"_comment\": \"Limiting factors\"}, {\"text\": \"Encourage the student to explain how photosynthesis and cellular respiration are interconnected and how photosynthesis fits into the wider carbon cycle.\", \"weight\": 10, \"_comment\": \"Respiration and carbon cycle\"}]',NULL),(57,'Photosynthesis (Dashboard Repro)','BRIEF:\nThis assessment evaluates the student\'s understanding of photosynthesis: its purpose, inputs/outputs, the two stages, pigments, limiting factors, and its link to respiration and the carbon cycle.\n\nRUBRIC:\nPhotosynthesis Rubric\n1. Core Process: Inputs and Outputs (30) - explains purpose, inputs (CO2, water, light), outputs (glucose, O2), ideally the balanced equation.\n2. Two Stages (25) - distinguishes light-dependent reactions (thylakoid) from the Calvin cycle (stroma) and links them.\n3. Pigments and Light Capture (20) - role of chlorophyll, why plants are green, fate of absorbed energy.\n4. Limiting Factors and Rate (15) - light intensity, CO2, temperature, concept of a limiting factor.\n5. Connection to Respiration / Carbon Cycle (10) - relates photosynthesis to respiration and the carbon cycle.\n\nSTUDENT:\n','manual','','',13);
