INSERT INTO `result` VALUES (1,'Q: Can you explain what GRNI (Goods Received Not Invoiced) is?\nA: Shira and I is basically when we receive goods but don\'t invoice for them. I don\'t know, I\'m not really an accounting person.\n\nQ: Why does GRNI exist in an FMCG business?\nA: I don\'t really know, you know, I\'m just a business guy.\n\nQ: [open floor]\nA: Nah, all seems pretty good to me. I\'m ready for my transcript.','Technical Accounting Understanding: 0/30 | Understanding of GRNI Releases: 0/20 | P&L Impact & Financial Insight: 0/20 | Analytical & Investigative Thinking: 0/15 | Commercial Awareness: 0/10 | Communication & Clarity: 1/5 || TOTAL: 1/100','Technical Accounting Understanding (0/30): The student did not demonstrate any understanding of GRNI, accruals, or the treatment of balance sheets versus P&L.\nUnderstanding of GRNI Releases (0/20): The student did not provide any explanation of GRNI releases or distinguish between valid and invalid uses.\nP&L Impact & Financial Insight (0/20): There was no discussion of how GRNI releases affect profit or any related financial insights.\nAnalytical & Investigative Thinking (0/15): The student did not demonstrate any analytical or investigative thinking regarding timing issues or other related topics.\nCommercial Awareness (0/10): The student did not connect GRNI to any real risks or commercial implications.\nCommunication & Clarity (1/5): The student communicated clearly but did not provide structured or relevant answers to the questions.\nOVERALL: The student did not demonstrate any understanding of the technical accounting concepts or their implications. There was a lack of engagement with the questions, and no relevant insights were provided. The communication was clear but lacked substance and structure.'),(2,'======================================================================\n  INTERVIEW TEST TRANSCRIPT\n  Profile : weak\n  Student : Patrick\n  Topic   : GRNI Accounting and P&L Risk Management in FMCG Finance\n======================================================================\n\n  INTERVIEWER:\n    Good afternoon, Patrick. Thank you for joining me today. To begin our discussion, can you explain what GRNI (Goods Received Not Invoiced) is?\n\n  STUDENT:\n    GRNI stands for Goods Received Not Invoiced, which means it\'s when you get goods but haven\'t received the invoice for them yet. It\'s important for keeping track of inventory and expenses.\n\n  INTERVIEWER:\n    Patrick, can you explain the role of GRNI in ensuring accurate financial reporting within FMCG finance?\n\n  STUDENT:\n    GRNI helps track goods received but not invoiced, which is important for accurate financial reporting. It ensures expenses are recorded correctly in FMCG finance.\n\n  INTERVIEWER:\n    Can you explain how GRNI is recorded in the financial statements, particularly in relation to accruals and the balance sheet versus the P&L treatment?\n\n  STUDENT:\n    GRNI is recorded as a liability on the balance sheet because it represents goods received but not yet invoiced. It doesn\'t affect the P&L until the invoice is received and matched with the expense.\n\n  INTERVIEWER:\n    Patrick, could you explain how the initial recognition of GRNI impacts the balance sheet and whether there is any immediate effect on the profit and loss statement?\n\n  STUDENT:\n    The initial recognition of GRNI affects the balance sheet by increasing inventory and accounts payable. There\'s no immediate effect on the profit and loss statement, but it will show up when the costs are recognized later.\n\n  INTERVIEWER:\n    Patrick, can you explain how a GRNI release specifically affects profit, and what implications this might have for earnings reporting?\n\n  STUDENT:\n    A GRNI release can affect profit by decreasing liabilities, which may lead to an increase in reported earnings. This could impact earnings reporting, making it seem like the company is more profitable than it actually is.\n\n  INTERVIEWER:\n    Patrick, could you elaborate on what specific risks arise if GRNI releases are used aggressively?\n\n  STUDENT:\n    If GRNI releases are used aggressively, it can lead to earnings manipulation, making financials look better than they are. This could result in poor decision-making based on inaccurate data.\n\n  INTERVIEWER:\n    Patrick, could you elaborate on how GRNI mismanagement specifically impacts decision-making in a commercial finance team?\n\n  STUDENT:\n    GRNI mismanagement can lead to inaccurate financial reporting, which affects decision-making in finance teams. This can result in poor budgeting, cash flow issues, and ultimately financial losses.\n\n  INTERVIEWER:\n    Patrick, can you explain how you would differentiate between valid and invalid GRNI releases in your investigation?\n\n  STUDENT:\n    I would look at the documentation and check if the transactions match with what we received and invoiced. Valid ones have proper paperwork, while invalid ones don\'t.\n\n  INTERVIEWER:\n    Patrick, can you elaborate on the specific controls you would implement to prevent incorrect GRNI releases?\n\n  STUDENT:\n    I think some controls could be to double-check the entries and have a verification step in the process. This would help avoid mistakes, but I\'m not sure about the specifics.\n\n  INTERVIEWER:\n    Patrick, could you elaborate on why GRNI is particularly important in FMCG businesses?\n\n  STUDENT:\n    GRNI is important in FMCG because it helps manage inventory and ensure accurate financial reporting. It relates to financial operations by affecting the balance sheet and cash flow.\n\n  INTERVIEWER:\n    Patrick, could you elaborate on how high volumes and fast inventory turnover specifically affect the accuracy of GRNI accounting?\n\n  STUDENT:\n    High volumes and fast inventory turnover can lead to mistakes in GRNI accounting because everything moves so quickly. This can create risks like inaccurate financial reporting or inventory shortages.','GRNI Definition: 3/5 | P&L Impact: 6/10 | Controls and Risk Management: 4/10 | FMCG Context: 4/5 || TOTAL: 17/30','GRNI Definition (3/5): Patrick provided a basic definition of GRNI, mentioning its role in tracking goods and expenses. However, he did not include the accrual context or its treatment on the balance sheet.\nP&L Impact (6/10): Patrick explained that GRNI releases can affect profit by decreasing liabilities and potentially inflating earnings. However, his explanation lacked detail on timing distortion and earnings risk.\nControls and Risk Management (4/10): Patrick mentioned generic controls like double-checking entries but did not provide GRNI-specific details or comprehensive reasoning tied to GRNI risks.\nFMCG Context (4/5): Patrick acknowledged the impact of high volumes and fast turnover on GRNI complexity, linking it to risks in financial reporting and inventory management.\nOVERALL: Patrick demonstrated a basic understanding of GRNI and its implications in FMCG finance. While he could explain some aspects of P&L impact and FMCG context, his responses lacked depth, particularly in controls and risk management. Further study on GRNI-specific controls and the detailed impact on financial statements would enhance his understanding.'),(3,'Section 1: Ask the student to explain how macroeconomics and microeconomics differ, particularly in terms of their focus on individual behavior versus aggregate economic activity. Encourage them to provide examples of how these concepts interrelate.\n\n[BOT] Good afternoon, [298760093]. Thank you for joining me today for this oral assessment. To begin, could you please explain how macroeconomics and microeconomics differ, particularly in terms of their focus on individual behavior versus aggregate economic activity? Additionally, I encourage you to provide examples of how these concepts interrelate.\n\n[STU] Well, basically, it\'s kind of like how the sum of all the individual actions makes up the group action.\n\n[BOT] Could you elaborate on how individual actions in microeconomics can influence broader economic indicators, such as GDP or inflation, in macroeconomics?\n\n[STU] Well, if people are more willing to buy stuff at a higher price, that can lead to inflation, or alternatively, if people are paid more, then they\'re probably going to spend more, which can also lead to inflation. If people are more productive at work, that increases GDP.\n\n[BOT] Can you explain the mechanisms through which increased consumer spending and productivity translate into higher GDP, and how these might also interact with inflation in a cyclical manner?\n\n[STU] Well, basically, as people spend more and make more, it\'s kind of more going into the GDP. And the more GDP you have, the more people make, the higher prices people will be willing to pay, and also the higher costs that are to make goods. So companies put up the prices, which means people have to spend more money, they have to spend more money, they\'re gonna demand higher wages, they demand higher wages, the companies put up the prices, and so on and so forth and so on and so forth. You get what\'s called a wage price spiral. And basically that means we get quite high inflation, eventually in theory.\n\n\nSection 2: Prompt the student to discuss what GDP measures, including its components (consumption, investment, government purchases, net exports) and why GDP is a critical indicator of economic health.\n\n[BOT] Can you explain what GDP measures, including its components such as consumption, investment, government purchases, and net exports? Additionally, why do you think GDP is considered a critical indicator of economic health?\n\n[STU] Well, GDP measures your consumer consumption, your consumer investment, your government spending, your balance of imports versus exports, and it\'s considered an indicator of economic health because it represents the volume of economic activity happening and the value being produced in a country. Obviously, there are lots of exceptions to this, but in general, the higher the GDP of the country is, the better off they\'re going to be. Like you look at some very, very poor countries in Africa, for example, they might have GDP in the range of 300 to 500 US dollars per person per year, and then you look at somewhere like, I don\'t know, Spain, which has like 25,000 US dollars per year. Obviously, focusing purely on increasing GDP isn\'t going to fix all of the country\'s problems, but if the GDP is too low, there\'s not really much they can do.\n\n[BOT] Can you elaborate on how changes in one of the components of GDP, such as consumer spending, might impact overall economic health, and what potential implications this could have for policy decisions?\n\n[STU] Well, basically if consumer spending goes up, it\'s gonna artificially pad your GDP, potentially while making people less prepared for economic shocks. So we might have politicians that are more inclined to encourage consumer spending at all times in order to artificially pad the GDP, when in fact, if we do have that, then you might be sort of not so prepared for future economic shocks.\n\n[BOT] How do you think policymakers could balance the need for stimulating consumer spending with the necessity of preparing for potential economic downturns?\n\n[STU] I think the main thing that they can do is to encourage balance, so basically to tell people not to spend beyond their means, but to still go out and spend money. The main tool they have for this is around inflation expectations. So if people think that there\'s going to be hyperinflation in the future, they\'re going to spend way, way, way too much and they\'re not going to save, conversely, if people think we\'re going to have disinflation in the future, then they\'re not going to be inclined to spend it all because their money is going to be worth more in the future. So in order to encourage balanced consumer spending, we have to sort of enforce a public perception of a low and stable inflation rate.\n\n\nSection 3: Inquire about the factors that contributed to high inflation in the 1970s and the reasons for its decline in subsequent decades. Encourage them to reference specific historical events or policies.\n\n[BOT] Can you discuss the factors that contributed to high inflation in the 1970s and the reasons for its decline in subsequent decades? Please reference specific historical events or policies that you think played a crucial role in this context.\n\n[STU] I don\'t really know what happened in the 1970s, like I know we had the sort of post-war boom, but like in the 70s maybe the oil crisis might have played a role because it just pushed up the prices of basically everything, but beyond that I wouldn\'t know really, sorry.\n\n[BOT] Can you elaborate on how the oil crisis specifically affected inflation rates and perhaps identify any government responses to that crisis that might have aimed to stabilize the economy?\n\n[STU] Well, with the oil crisis, the price of oil and subsequently petrol and sort of all petrochemicals went up. The problem with that and the reason why it\'s skyrocketed inflation is that especially in the 70s, you needed oil for basically any sort of industrial activity and especially for transport of finished goods. So if your price of trucking goes up, your price of every single thing that needs to go on a truck also goes up, which means we had pretty sustained economy-wide inflation and I don\'t really know how they might have stabilized it.\n\n\nSection 4: Ask the student to explain what budget and trade deficits are, why a country might run high deficits, and the potential consequences of these deficits on the economy.\n\n[BOT] Can you explain what budget and trade deficits are, why a country might run high deficits, and the potential consequences of these deficits on the economy?\n\n[STU] Like a budget or a trade deficit for a budget deficit, it\'s when you spend more than you make, so it could be a put period of reinvestment. And then a trade deficit is basically when you buy more imports than you sell exports. The downside of a budget deficit is that you end up with debt, basically, and you end up having to make that up in a future years. The downside of a trade deficit is that you can seriously devalue your currency because you don\'t have sufficient international demand for your currency and you\'re putting a lot of it out there by buying imports.\n\n[BOT] Can you elaborate on how a country might manage its budget and trade deficits to mitigate the potential negative impacts you mentioned, such as debt accumulation and currency devaluation?\n\n[STU] Well, the key for budget deficits is to over time maintain an approximately balanced budget. So you might have certain years where you need to make big investments, but you have to balance that with years that you run a budget surplus to balance the books. And you need to make sure that your total debt doesn\'t reach a level where the cost of servicing it wipes out any potential future budget surplus. For currency devaluation, that\'s a trickier one. There are certain cases like China where they deliberately devalue their currency to make their exports more appealing. And then they shift into a portion of trade surplus, whereas I don\'t know, somewhere like the USA relies on other non-trade measures such as financial markets to keep its currency afloat.\n\n\nSection 5: Encourage the student to describe the steps involved in developing an economic model, including documenting facts, developing the model, and testing predictions. They should provide an example of a model they learned about and its relevance.\n\n[BOT] Can you describe the steps involved in developing an economic model, including documenting facts, developing the model, and testing predictions? Additionally, please provide an example of a model you learned about and discuss its relevance.\n\n[STU] I don\'t really know any of that and I\'d like to move on. We don\'t have much time left.\n\n\nOpen floor\n\n[BOT] Is there anything you would like to add, 298760093, or is there any previous answer you would like to briefly elaborate on?\n\n[STU] not really i think i\'ve kind of said everything that i need to\n','Understanding of Customer-First Strategy: 1/4 | Application of Real-World Examples: 1/4 | Recognition of Technology-First Pitfall: 1/4 | Strategic Vision and Cohesion: 1/4 | Critical Engagement with Source Material: 1/4 || TOTAL: 5/20','Understanding of Customer-First Strategy (1/4): The student did not address the customer-first strategy or its implications, focusing instead on economic concepts unrelated to the rubric.\nApplication of Real-World Examples (1/4): The student did not use examples from Amazon or Apple, nor did they relate any examples to strategic concepts from the lecture.\nRecognition of Technology-First Pitfall (1/4): There was no discussion of the technology-first pitfall, as the student focused on economic topics rather than strategic pitfalls.\nStrategic Vision and Cohesion (1/4): The student did not demonstrate an understanding of strategic vision or cohesion, as their responses were centered on economic principles.\nCritical Engagement with Source Material (1/4): The student did not engage with the lecture content or critically analyze strategic concepts, focusing instead on economic theories.\nOVERALL: The student\'s responses were focused on economic concepts rather than the strategic principles outlined in the rubric. There was no engagement with the customer-first strategy, real-world examples, or the technology-first pitfall. The student did not demonstrate an understanding of strategic vision or critically engage with the lecture material. Future assessments should ensure alignment with the rubric\'s focus on strategic thinking in information systems.'),(4,'Section 1: Ask the student to explain the overall purpose of photosynthesis, including the main inputs and outputs and, if they can, the overall balanced equation.\n\n[BOT] Good morning, TEST STUDENT. I appreciate you taking the time to meet today. To begin our discussion, could you please explain the overall purpose of photosynthesis, including the main inputs and outputs? If you are able, I would also like you to share the overall balanced equation for the process.\n\n[STU] photosynthesis is basically how plants take sunlight and CO2 and convert that into oxygen and energy. I don\'t really know like the balanced chemical equation, but it seems like they use it to basically create energy for themselves given that they don\'t like eat.\n\n[BOT] Could you elaborate on how the process of photosynthesis contributes to the energy needs of the plant and why the outputs, such as oxygen and glucose, are important for both the plant and the environment?\n\n[STU] Well, the oxygen output is important, because otherwise, if we didn\'t have anything but a synthesizing, we would end up with way too much CO2 in the atmosphere, which is just not conducive to life for humans. And then the glucose is important for the plant because it\'s their main source of energy to grow and reproduce.\n\n[BOT] Can you explain how the process of photosynthesis helps maintain the balance of carbon dioxide and oxygen in the atmosphere, particularly in relation to the role of plants in ecosystems?\n\n[STU] Well plants serve as carbon sinks in ecosystems, so when animals respite and do their thing they\'re basically turning oxygen into CO2, they\'re sort of excreting CO2 from the body, and then plants do the other side of that equation. They take that CO2 and turn it back into oxygen that can then be used by the animals, so it\'s all a bit of a cyclical process.\n\n\nSection 2: Prompt the student to describe the two main stages of photosynthesis - the light-dependent reactions and the Calvin cycle - including where in the chloroplast each takes place and how they are connected.\n\n[BOT] Can you describe the two main stages of photosynthesis—the light-dependent reactions and the Calvin cycle—specifically detailing where in the chloroplast each stage occurs and how the two stages are interconnected?\n\n[STU] I really don\'t know man, I stopped taking bio in year 10.\n\n[BOT] What specific aspects of photosynthesis do you find most confusing or challenging, and how do you think they might relate to the overall process?\n\n[STU] I mean, I don\'t really understand how the chemistry of it works, like how we turning oxygen into CO2 and back again. Like, doesn\'t that take energy? And I thought the whole point was we got energy.\n\n[BOT] Can you elaborate on your understanding of the overall balanced equation for photosynthesis, including what happens to carbon dioxide and water during the process?\n\n[STU] Well, basically the plants take carbon dioxide and water and then turn that into hydrocarbon chains, be that glucose or like carbohydrates, whatever you want to call it. And also they are just storing like raw carbon in them and then they emit the oxygen so they use up water in CO2 and they output oxygen and glucose.\n\n\nSection 3: Inquire about the role of chlorophyll and other pigments in capturing light energy, why plants appear green, and what happens to the absorbed energy.\n\n[BOT] Can you elaborate on the role of chlorophyll and other pigments in capturing light energy, why plants appear green, and what happens to the absorbed energy during photosynthesis?\n\n[STU] chlorophyllus, the pigment that enables photosynthesis, chlorophyllus green. So any plant that photosynthesizes is going to be quite green. And the absorbed energy is not really absorbed, it\'s kind of converted into carbohydrates and then stored in the plant as well.\n\n[BOT] Can you explain how the process of converting absorbed light energy into carbohydrates occurs at the molecular level, particularly in relation to the electron transport chain?\n\n[STU] No, I cannot. Sorry, I don\'t really know.\n\n[BOT] What specific aspects of the electron transport chain or the conversion of light energy into carbohydrates do you find most challenging to understand?\n\n[STU] Well, light energies in photons, and I don\'t really understand how the photons are going to trigger any sort of chemical change.\n\n\nSection 4: Ask the student to discuss the factors that affect the rate of photosynthesis - such as light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration, and temperature - and what is meant by a limiting factor.\n\n[BOT] TEST STUDENT, could you discuss the factors that affect the rate of photosynthesis, such as light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration, and temperature? Additionally, what do you mean by a limiting factor in this context?\n\n[STU] basically the more light and carbon dioxide in the higher temperature, the more photosynthesis you\'re going to be. So like in a rainforest like the Amazon where you have lots of sun and lots of it\'s hot and there\'s lots of carbon dioxide around, you\'re going to get very intense photosynthesis plants can grow very quickly, they can store lots of energy and a limiting factor would be if you\'ve got like the correct temperature and the correct amount of light but not enough carbon dioxide or whatever. So an environment kind of needs the optimal balance of all three for optimal photosynthesis conditions.\n\n[BOT] Can you elaborate on how the absence of one of these factors, like carbon dioxide, could specifically impact the overall process of photosynthesis, and what might happen to the plant\'s growth in that scenario?\n\n[STU] Like if you don\'t have enough carbon dioxide, you can\'t do the reaction that many times that carbon dioxide is like your fuel. And if you run out of fuel, you might have optimal conditions otherwise, but you\'re just not gonna get the plant to grow because you don\'t have the building blocks for the glucose that it needs. A similar thing could happen if you don\'t have enough sunlight. Like the sunlight kind of starts the engine and keeps it going. And even if you\'ve got lots of fuel, if you don\'t have a starter, then it\'s not gonna work.\n\n[BOT] Could you explain how the light-dependent reactions specifically contribute to the overall process of photosynthesis and how they are linked to the Calvin cycle?\n\n[STU] Not really, no. I don\'t know what the Calvin cycle is.\n\n\nSection 5: Encourage the student to explain how photosynthesis and cellular respiration are interconnected and how photosynthesis fits into the wider carbon cycle.\n\n[BOT] Can you explain how photosynthesis and cellular respiration are interconnected and how photosynthesis fits into the wider carbon cycle?\n\n[STU] Well, cellular respiration takes oxygen and glucose and turns it into ATP and carbon dioxide. Then whatever organism is doing, that respiration will emit that carbon dioxide, maybe via the lungs if it\'s a mammal. And then photosynthesis takes that carbon dioxide along with water and sunlight and turns it back into oxygen and glucose, which are the things that the other organisms need to make ATP. So it kind of all goes random and cycle. The plants take carbon, make oxygen, the other guys take oxygen, make carbon.\n\n[BOT] Can you elaborate on how the processes of photosynthesis and cellular respiration complement each other in terms of energy flow within an ecosystem?\n\n[STU] Well, photosynthesis acts to introduce energy into the ecosystem through the sun\'s light and then cellular respiration serves to take that stored energy from the sun. So the glucose from the plants and turn that into movement or new tissue or growth or birth or whatever. So it basically means photosynthesis means that the sun can keep the ecosystem going.\n\n[BOT] Can you explain in more detail how the energy stored in glucose during photosynthesis is utilized by organisms during cellular respiration, and what happens to that energy throughout the process?\n\n[STU] Well, basically when cells break down glucose, they\'re doing that to make ATP, which is like the energy that our body can use inside it. It\'s the thing that makes muscles contract and all of that sort of thing. So it goes from the chemical potential energy of glucose to another form of chemical potential energy, which is ATP, then to either kinetic energy, to heat energy, whatever, in order to do that.\n\n\nOpen floor\n\n[BOT] TEST STUDENT, is there anything you would like to add or any previous answer you would like to briefly elaborate on?\n\n[STU] I\'m okay, thank you. I think I\'ve said everything I know.\n','Core Process: Inputs and Outputs: 15/30 | Two Stages: Light-Dependent Reactions and the Calvin Cycle: 5/25 | Role of Pigments and Light Capture: 5/20 | Limiting Factors and Rate: 10/15 | Connection to Respiration and the Carbon Cycle: 8/10 || TOTAL: 43/100','Core Process: Inputs and Outputs (15/30): The student identified some inputs and outputs of photosynthesis but did not provide the balanced chemical equation. Their understanding of the purpose of photosynthesis was basic and lacked detail.\nTwo Stages: Light-Dependent Reactions and the Calvin Cycle (5/25): The student was unable to describe the two main stages of photosynthesis or their locations within the chloroplast. They expressed confusion about the chemistry involved.\nRole of Pigments and Light Capture (5/20): The student mentioned chlorophyll but did not accurately explain its role or why plants appear green. They struggled with the concept of energy conversion at the molecular level.\nLimiting Factors and Rate (10/15): The student correctly identified factors affecting photosynthesis and explained the concept of limiting factors, but their explanation lacked depth and specific examples.\nConnection to Respiration and the Carbon Cycle (8/10): The student demonstrated a good understanding of the interconnection between photosynthesis and cellular respiration, as well as their roles in the carbon cycle.\nOVERALL: The student showed a basic understanding of photosynthesis, particularly in relation to its role in the carbon cycle and limiting factors. However, they struggled with the detailed biochemical processes and the specific roles of pigments. Further study on the stages of photosynthesis and the molecular mechanisms involved would be beneficial.');
