sion and Cohesion            15%     ___           ___\n5. Critical Engagement with Source Material 10%     ___           ___\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTOTAL                                      100%                   ___  / 4.0\n\nGrade conversion (if applicable):\n  3.5 – 4.0  →  A  (Excellent)\n  2.75 – 3.49 → B  (Proficient)\n  2.0 – 2.74 →  C  (Developing)\n  Below 2.0  →  D/F (Beginning)\n\n================================================\nASSESSOR NOTES\n--------------\n[Space for written feedback]\n\n\n\n\n================================================\n\n\nSTUDENT:\n'),(57,'BRIEF:\nRUBRIC: INFOSYS 321 Lecture Content Evaluation\nCourse: INFOSYS 321 | Lecture: L01C | Date: Wed 23 Jul\n================================================\n\nOVERVIEW\n--------\nThis rubric is designed to evaluate student understanding of the key concepts\npresented in this lecture, which covers customer-centric strategy, technology\ncommercialisation, and the use of industry examples (Amazon, Apple) to illustrate\nstrategic thinking in information systems.\n\n================================================\nCRITERION 1: Understanding of Customer-First Strategy\nWeight: 30%\n\n4 - Excellent\n    Clearly articulates the principle \"start with the customer experience and work\n    backwards to the technology.\" Can explain why reversing this order (starting\n    with technology) is problematic, with reference to lecture content or examples.\n\n3 - Proficient\n    Demonstrates solid understanding of the customer-first principle and can\n    distinguish it from a technology-first approach, with minor gaps in explanation.\n\n2 - Developing\n    Shows basic awareness that customer experience matters but struggles to explain\n    the directionality (customer → technology) or why the reverse fails.\n\n1 - Beginning\n    Little to no understanding of the customer-first strategic principle. May\n    conflate customer focus with marketing or