mer-first strategic principle. May\n    conflate customer focus with marketing or product features.\n\n------------------------------------------------\nCRITERION 2: Application of Real-World Examples\nWeight: 25%\n\n4 - Excellent\n    Accurately uses the Amazon and/or Apple examples from the lecture to support\n    arguments. Understands that Amazon illustrates customer convenience/reach and\n    Apple illustrates vision-driven strategy rooted in customer benefit.\n\n3 - Proficient\n    References at least one example correctly and links it meaningfully to the\n    strategic concept being discussed.\n\n2 - Developing\n    Mentions examples but the connection to the strategic argument is surface-level\n    or partially inaccurate.\n\n1 - Beginning\n    Does not use examples, or uses them incorrectly/out of context.\n\n------------------------------------------------\nCRITERION 3: Recognition of the Technology-First Pitfall\nWeight: 20%\n\n4 - Excellent\n    Can explain the \"technology-first\" mistake — building technology and then trying\n    to find a market — and why it fails to produce a cohesive, scalable vision.\n    May reference the speaker\'s self-acknowledged experience with this mistake.\n\n3 - Proficient\n    Understands the pitfall and can describe it, though explanation may lack depth\n    or miss the scale/commercialisation dimension ($8–10B product vision).\n\n2 - Developing\n    Vaguely aware that starting with technology can be problematic but cannot fully\n    explain why or connect it to business outcomes.\n\n1 - Beginning\n    Does not identify or understand the technology-first pitfall.\n\n------------------------------------------------\nCRITERION 4: Strategic Vision and Cohesion\nWeight: 15%\n\n4 - Excellent\n    Understands that a strategy must be cohesive and scalable — not just technically\n    impressive in demos or niche applications. Can discuss what makes a vision\n    \"larger\" and commercially viable.\n\n3 - Proficient\n    Shows aw